.TH GGE\-CAMERA 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-camera \- world versus screen coordinates, following, bounds, zoom, shake
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \fBCamera\fR (camera.h) is a window onto a world larger than the screen. It holds a world position that appears at the center of the view, a zoom, the view size, optional world bounds, and an offset for shake. Two conversions do all the work: \fBcamera_to_screen\fR for drawing and \fBcamera_to_world\fR for the mouse. Games whose whole world fits on screen (puzzle, card, single\-screen arcade) do not need one.
.PP
.SH SETUP
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static Camera cam;                                     /* presentation state */
static void on_enter(Engine *e, Scene *s) {
  GameState *g = e\->user;
  camera_init(&cam, e\->logical_w, e\->logical_h);      /* zoom 1, no bounds, at the origin */
  camera_set_bounds(&cam, 0, 0, map.w * map.tile, map.h * map.tile);
  camera_follow(&cam, g\->player.pos, 0, 0);            /* smoothing 0: snap to the player now */
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
The camera is derived from state, so it lives in a scene static and is rebuilt in on_enter; nothing about it is saved.
.PP
.SH FOLLOWING
Call camera_follow(3) every tick, after the target moved:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static void update(Engine *e, Scene *s, float dt) {
  ... move the player ...
  camera_follow(&cam, rect_center(player_hitbox(&g\->player)), 0.15f, dt);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBsmoothing\fR is a time constant in seconds: 0 snaps, 0.1 to 0.3 is a tight follow that still smooths out steps, 0.5 to 1.0 drifts lazily. The motion is frame\-rate independent (it uses smooth_t(3)). Follow the center of the hitbox, not the top\-left, or the player sits off\-center.
.PP
Variations, all done by choosing the target:
.TP
\fBLook\-ahead\fR
target = player + facing * 40, so the player sees more of where they are going.
.TP
\fBDead zone\fR
only move the target when the player leaves a box around the current camera position: compute the box in world space from \fBcam.pos\fR, and set target to push the box edge.
.TP
\fBRoom\-locked (Zelda)\fR
target = center of the room the player is in; a large smoothing (0.3) glides between rooms, or 0 snaps.
.TP
\fBVertical only on landing\fR
keep target.y at the last ground height until on_ground; platformers feel steadier.
.TP
\fBTwo targets (versus)\fR
target = midpoint of the players; zoom out when they are far apart.
.TP
\fBCinematic\fR
tween \fBcam.pos\fR directly from a cutscene CUT_DO callback (gge\-cutscene(7)); stop calling camera_follow while it plays.
.SH BOUNDS
camera_set_bounds(3) gives the world rectangle the camera may show; camera_follow clamps so the view never leaves it. A world smaller than the view pins to the top\-left; for a level narrower than the screen, center it by drawing with a fixed offset instead of a camera, or pad the map. Bounds are applied only inside camera_follow; setting \fBcam.pos\fR directly bypasses them.
.PP
.SH CONVERTING
.TP
\fBcamera_to_screen(c, world)\fR
where to draw a world point, in logical pixels.
.TP
\fBcamera_rect(c, r)\fR
a world rectangle on screen, scaled by zoom (hitboxes, world\-space UI).
.TP
\fBcamera_to_world(c, screen)\fR
the world point under a screen position (the mouse).
.TP
\fBcamera_visible(c)\fR
the world rectangle on screen, for culling.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/* drawing an entity */
Vec2 s = camera_to_screen(&cam, en\->pos);
sprite_draw_scaled(&sheet, frame, s.x, s.y, cam.zoom, 0, flip);
/* the tile under the mouse */
Vec2 w = camera_to_world(&cam, v2(in\->mouse_x, in\->mouse_y));
int tx = (int)floorf(w.x / map.tile), ty = (int)floorf(w.y / map.tile);
/* skip what is off screen */
if (!rect_overlaps(camera_visible(&cam), enemy_hitbox(en))) continue;
.EE
.RE
.PP
Draw with \fBsprite_draw_scaled(..., cam.zoom, ...)\fR when zoom is not 1, so sprites scale with the world; tilemap_draw(3) does this itself.
.PP
.SH ZOOM
\fBcam.zoom\fR scales the world about the view center: 2 shows half as much world at twice the size. Integer zooms keep pixel art crisp. Change it with the wheel or a key, and clamp it: \fBcam.zoom = clampf(cam.zoom + in\->wheel * 0.5f, 1, 4)\fR. Bounds account for zoom automatically. HUD elements are not zoomed because they do not go through the camera.
.PP
.SH SHAKE
Shake through \fBoffset\fR, never through \fBpos\fR, so following and bounds are untouched:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static Shake shake;
/* on impact */          shake_start(&shake, 6, 20);
/* every tick */         shake_update(&shake, dt); cam.offset = v2(shake.ox, shake.oy);
.EE
.RE
.PP
See gge\-tween(7) for Shake.
.PP
.SH SMOOTH SCROLLING
The camera moves in update, once per tick, so with interpolation\-drawn entities (gge\-drawing(7)) the camera itself may step visibly on high\-refresh displays. Interpolate it too: keep \fBprev_cam\fR in a static, set it at the top of update, and in render use a copy of the camera whose \fBpos\fR is \fBv2_lerp(prev_cam, cam.pos, alpha)\fR for all conversions:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
Camera view = cam; view.pos = v2_lerp(prev_cam, cam.pos, alpha);
Vec2 s = camera_to_screen(&view, ...);
.EE
.RE
.PP
.SH PARALLAX
Backgrounds that move slower than the world are drawn with a scaled camera position: \fBfloat bx = \-view.pos.x * 0.5f;\fR and the far background at \fBbx\fR modulo its width, tiled twice to cover the screen. Layers at 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 give depth.
.PP
.SH SPLIT SCREEN
Two cameras with \fBview_w = e\->logical_w / 2\fR, drawn one after the other with an x offset added to the results of camera_to_screen for the second. There is no clipping, so draw the second half's background rectangle first and keep sprites inside by culling with camera_visible.
.PP
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
Updating the camera in render: it then lags one frame and is not interpolated correctly. Update it in update.
.IP \(bu 2
Following a top\-left position: the player appears in the lower\-right quadrant. Follow the center.
.IP \(bu 2
Forgetting the camera for one kind of thing: it stays fixed on screen while the world scrolls. Every world\-space draw goes through the conversion; every HUD draw does not.
.IP \(bu 2
Setting bounds smaller than the view: the view pins to the corner; make the level at least a screen in each dimension or center it manually.
